module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_killed_run_leaves_its_credential_file_behind

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_killed_run_leaves_its_credential_file_behind: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_killed_run_leaves_its_credential_file_behind" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "**a killed run leaves its credential file behind** (a run holds a credential in a file because its transport reads one, and removes the file on every path out of the bracket. A process that is killed reaches no path out, so the file survives the run. Every arm calls the shred, which is exactly why the residue is invisible in review)." as NonEmptyStr,

    "INVALID STATE: a run that holds a credential in a file and removes it on every path OUT of the bracket still leaves the file when the process does not reach any path out -- SIGKILL, an OOM kill, a timeout that kills rather than signals. The remedy every author reaches for (shred on success AND on each failure arm) is complete with respect to the code's control flow and silent with respect to its termination, so the residue is invisible in review: every arm does call the shred." as NonEmptyStr,

    "INSTANCE: gunbc.fleet_health_observe observe_host_health places the health-reader credential through gunbc.auth.netrc_binding place_netrc_binding_privately (mktemp dir 0700, file 0600) and shreds it on every return, refusing the host's observation when the shred fails. On 2026-09-19 a fleet run was killed by its own `timeout 1700` during srv4 and left /tmp/tmp.jxNqTwXnnN/netrc holding the srv4 reader password on srv1 until it was removed by hand. The same shape is reachable from gunbc.tools.bmc_onboard and gunbc.tools.bmc_health_reader_converge, whose netrc path is a fixed name rather than a private directory." as NonEmptyStr,

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the credential is in a FILE because the transport reads one (curl --netrc-file), and the file must outlive the call that reads it. A predictable path is not the repair: it trades an unbounded-lifetime residue for a symlink target in a shared directory. The residue window is the process lifetime, and the exposure is bounded by directory mode 0700 plus file mode 0600 -- an operator-account-readable secret on a host the operator already administers, not a fleet-wide one." as NonEmptyStr,

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The shred is real and runs on every modeled path; nothing makes the residue unwritable, and no check observes it. CEILING: structurally impossible for this class -- a credential the transport reads without a filesystem path (a file descriptor the child inherits, or a credential handed to an in-process HTTP client) has no file to leave behind. That ceiling is attainable only for a transport that accepts one; curl --netrc-file does not, so today's honest ceiling is mechanically preventable: a modeled run binding whose cleanup is owned by the supervisor rather than the process (a systemd unit with ExecStopPost, or a scope whose teardown removes the private directory) removes the file even when the process is killed." as NonEmptyStr,

    "NEXT TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY: an execution binding that owns credential-file teardown independently of process exit, SUFFICIENT FOR the health route and both BMC tools to place a credential file whose removal does not depend on the process reaching a return. A sweep at the next run's start is NOT that capability: it shortens the window to the gap between runs and leaves it unbounded when the next run never comes.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
